-- title:
--   Section ideal of a point in the smooth locus is invertible
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring, $C$ a scheme and $c : C \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a separated morphism. Let $V$ be an open subscheme of $C$ such that the composite of the inclusion $V \hookrightarrow C$ with $c$ is smooth of relative dimension $1$. Let $T$ be a scheme, $t : T \to \operatorname{Spec} R$ a morphism, and $a : T \to C$ a morphism with $a$ followed by $c$ equal to $t$, whose image on points satisfies $\operatorname{range}(a_{\mathrm{base}}) \subseteq V$. Write $X := C \times_{\operatorname{Spec} R} T$ and let $\gamma : T \to X$ be the graph of $a$, that is the morphism induced by $a$ and $\mathrm{id}_T$; the divisor `RelEffCartierDiv.ofPoint c a ha` has underlying ideal sheaf datum $\mathcal I := \ker \gamma$. The conclusion is that $\mathcal I$ satisfies the project's predicate `IsInvertible`: for every point $x$ of $X$ there are an affine open $U \subseteq X$ and $f \in \Gamma(X, U)$ with $x$ in the basic open $X_f$, together with a non-zero-divisor $g \in \Gamma(X, \operatorname{affineBasicOpen} f)$ such that $\mathcal I(\operatorname{affineBasicOpen} f)$ is the ideal generated by $g$.
--
--   This is the statement that a section of a relative curve whose image lies in the locus where the structure morphism is smooth of relative dimension $1$ cuts out an effective Cartier divisor, with the smoothness required only on an open subscheme containing the image rather than on all of $C$. It is used in the analysis of ideal sheaves of sections on models of modular curves, where the divisor attached to a $T$-point is compared with a product of powers of other section divisors.
